\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Recall that the Coleman-Liau index is computed using 
\f1\fs28 index = 0.0588 * L - 0.296 * S - 15.8
\f0\fs32 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\fs28 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
L
\f0\fs32  is the average number of letters per 100 words in the text: that is, the number of letters divided by the number of words, all multiplied by 100.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\fs28 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
S
\f0\fs32  is the average number of sentences per 100 words in the text: that is, the number of sentences divided by the number of words, all multiplied by 100.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You\'92ll want to round the result to the nearest whole number, so recall that 
\f1\fs28 round
\f0\fs32  is declared in 
\f1\fs28 math.h
\f0\fs32 , per {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://manual.cs50.io/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 manual.cs50.io}}.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Recall that, when dividing values of type 
\f1\fs28 int
\f0\fs32  in C, the result will also be an 
\f1\fs28 int
\f0\fs32 , with any remainder (i.e., digits after the decimal point) discarded. Put another way, the result will be \'93truncated.\'94 You might want to cast your one or more values to 
\f1\fs28 float
\f0\fs32  before performing division when calculating 
\f1\fs28 L
\f0\fs32  and 
\f1\fs28 S
\f0\fs32 !\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If the resulting index number is 16 or higher (equivalent to or greater than a senior undergraduate reading level), your program should output \'93Grade 16+\'94 instead of outputting an exact index number. If the index number is less than 1, your program should output \'93Before Grade 1\'94
